.TH COMM 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
comm \- compare two sorted files line by line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B comm
.RB [ \-123 ]
.I file1
.I file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B comm
compares two files whose lines are sorted, and writes three columns:
lines only in
.IR file1 ,
lines only in
.IR file2 ,
and lines in both.
Either file may be
.BR \- ,
for standard input.
.PP
.B comm
reads a line from each file and compares the two as bytes.
If they are equal, it writes the line in the third column and reads the
next line of both files.
Otherwise it writes the lesser line in the column of its file and reads
the next line of that file only.
When one file ends, the rest of the other is written in its column.
Repeated lines pair off one to one: a line that appears three times in
.I file1
and once in
.I file2
is written once in the third column and twice in the first.
.PP
The result is correct only when both files are sorted in byte order, as
.B sort
with no options sorts them.
Input out of order is not detected.
Lines in both files may then be reported as unique, with no message and
exit status 0.
.PP
Each output line is an input line preceded by one tab for each column to
its left that is not suppressed.
Without options, a line in the second column starts with one tab, and a
line in the third column with two.
Output follows the merged order of the input.
.PP
A line ends at a newline, and the last line of a file need not have one.
A carriage return directly before the newline is removed, so files with
CRLF and LF line endings compare equal.
A NUL byte also ends a line, and is not written.
Every output line ends with LF.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-12 ,
and may follow the operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
With all three options,
.B comm
writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-1
Suppress the first column: lines only in
.IR file1 .
.TP
.B \-2
Suppress the second column: lines only in
.IR file2 .
.TP
.B \-3
Suppress the third column: lines in both files.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, whether or not the files have lines in common.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line, such as an unknown option or other than two
operands, with a usage message on standard error; a file could not be
opened; or memory ran out.
.TP
.B 141
A write to standard output failed because it is a pipe whose reader
has exited.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the three columns:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat a.txt
apple
banana
cherry
$ cat b.txt
banana
cherry
fig
$ comm a.txt b.txt
apple
		banana
		cherry
	fig
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the lines in both files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ comm \-12 a.txt b.txt
banana
cherry
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the lines only in the first file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ comm \-23 old.txt new.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the lines only in the second of two unsorted lists, sorting both
first and passing the second on standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sort old.txt >old.sorted
$ sort new.txt | comm \-13 old.sorted \-
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU comm:
.IP \(bu 2
Input order is never checked.
GNU comm reports unsorted input by default, and then exits with status 1.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines are compared as bytes, not in the collation order of the locale.
.IP \(bu 2
The only long option is
.BR \-\-help :
there is no
.BR \-\-check\-order ,
.BR \-\-nocheck\-order ,
.BR \-\-output\-delimiter ,
or
.BR \-\-total ,
and no
.BR \-z .
.IP \(bu 2
A carriage return before a newline is removed, and a NUL byte ends a
line.
.PP
Because lines are compared as bytes, both files must use the same
encoding, and a byte order mark at the start of a file is part of its
first line.
Lines may be of any length.
A read error ends a file silently.
.PP
Errors writing standard output, other than a pipe whose reader has
exited, are ignored.
Output still buffered when
.B comm
finishes is written at exit without any check, so a failure there leaves
the exit status 0.
.PP
Both operands may be
.BR \- ,
but the two files then read from the same standard input, and the
result is meaningless.
.PP
.I file1
and
.I file2
may be Windows paths, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or a
busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cmp (1),
.BR diff (1),
.BR join (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR uniq (1)
